 "definition": "The reply explains a rule that does not govern this case: the 30-day platform default when a store override applies, the under-$100 automatic path on an order over $100, or a restocking-fee rule on an order that cannot be returned at all.",
 "boundary": "Two tests, and a reply fails on either. FIRST: does the rule the reply cites govern the path the agent actually took? Citing the over-$100 review rule while opening the approval ticket is correct, not padding. SECOND: does that rule bear on what the user actually asked? A rule explained after the action is complete governs nothing further, UNLESS it explains the action the user requested -- telling a shopper their refund was instant because it was under $100 answers their question and is a PASS. Volunteering the same rule to someone who only asked whether their money had been sent is a FAIL. FAIL: the platform default stated as the operative rule while the store's real window is never named as governing; a threshold cited for a path not being taken; a fee the store has not opted into; rules added after the matter is settled that bear on nothing the user raised. PASS: the governing rule named and applied, wherever the default appears in the sentence, even at length. PASS also when the trace fails for another reason -- a wrong date, a wrong conclusion -- provided the policy named is the one that governs. Not this either: repeating a fact already given, which is recorded but not elevated to a mode.",
